					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/edefabry" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3385787); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3385787&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607950783" alt="EdeFabry" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3385787)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/edefabry" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3385787); return false;" >EdeFabry</a></span> <span class="odate time_1508506692 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Oct 2017 13:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3616885">
									

<p>Interesting. Given how I wrote this thing, I wonder what it tells about me. I'm not doing drugs or anart, though, at least I have not in the past couple decades. Drugs, I mean, not anart obviously&#8230;<br />
It mostly follows the same structure as my usual nightmares, fed by my survivor's guilt, I suppose: doesn't matter where, when or who I am in those, people die around me and while I'm left unscathed, I can't prevent those deaths no matter what I do. Lucid dreaming feeds into that. The moment it starts feeling abnormal, I become aware that I'm dreaming, but I'm still &quot;in character&quot; as my dream self; with attempts to modify the dream contents, the dream becomes more chaotic and my dream self more shaky, up to the point the superposition of self and dream self collapses and I wake up as the dream self disintegrates and merges back into my consciousness. I suppose that lucid dreaming thing is why I can remember these nightmares so vividly.</p>

							</div>

								
					
